# Strategy Optimizer — Cursor Rules

## Project Context
Desktop application for optimizing TradingView Pine Script and Python trading strategies.
Users upload a strategy file (.pine or .py), configure parameter sweep ranges, and run
distributed parallel optimization across local network machines. Users can drill into any
result to see the full trade list and equity curve.

## Architecture
- Two Input Paths: Pine Script (.pine) gets translated to Python; Python (.py) runs directly
- Both paths feed into the same Python backtester engine
- Tauri (Rust) handles UI, file system, subprocess calls, distributed coordination
- Python handles backtesting, indicators, data fetching

## Stack
- Frontend: React 18 + Tailwind CSS (Tauri webview)
- Desktop Shell: Tauri v2 (Rust)
- Backtester: Python (numpy)
- Pine Translator: Rust (tokenizer → parser → AST → Python emitter)
- Data Fetchers: Python (crypto/futures/stocks)
- Distributed Sweep: Rust coordinator → workers via TCP

## Key Directories
- src/ — React frontend
- src-tauri/src/ — Rust backend
- src-tauri/src/pine/ — Pine Script translator
- src-tauri/python/ — Python backtester engine
- src-tauri/fetchers/ — Python data fetcher scripts
- strategies/ — User strategy files

## Important Lessons
- Windows file permissions fail in Program Files — use AppData
- Tauri shell plugin needs explicit scope in tauri.conf.json
- Pine input.float(10.0)/100 compiles differently than input.float(0.10)
- Binance spot vs futures APIs return different timestamps
- localStorage essential for UI state persistence across tab switches
- Create .env from PowerShell Set-Content, not from Cursor editor
